他在电影《浩劫》中看到了埃尔巴，然后心想，等等，那个人肯定写过我十几岁时看过的那份报告。当时，他在瑞典已经是一位非常杰出的癌症专家和科学家。他一路横渡到加拿大温哥华，位于加拿大西海岸，顺便说一句，这几乎是你在欧洲距离奥斯威辛最远的地方，去见那个他认为救了他一命的人。他踏上这段旅程，见到了韦尔伯，并说，正是因为你在这份报告中的内容，我才得以获救。我太感激了。不仅是我，还有我们之前谈到的那件事，布达佩斯的二十万人，你说你做了一件如此非凡、如此美妙的事情。

34
00:25:54.000 --> 00:26:37.000
And that's the note I tried to end the book on, that Gail Klein's children and great-grandchildren are alive, and so are the descendants of those 200,000, more, I write, than even Rudolf Verbe, who was so brilliant at memorizing the detail, more even than he could count. So I want people to take that away. But when Verbe himself heard that, he couldn't hear it. He just focused on the numbers he didn't save. And I've spoken to other people who've worked with or talked to Holocaust rescuers, and apparently this is a very common trait. The Nicholas Wintons of this world, you know, the British man who is credited with saving some 600 lives, all those people involved
这也是我试图为本书画上句号的音符，盖尔·克莱因的孩子和曾孙们都还活着，那二十万人的后代也是如此，甚至更多，我写道，比鲁道夫·韦尔伯还要多，他极其擅长背诵细节，甚至比他所能数出的还要多。所以我希望人们能记住这一点。但当韦尔伯本人听到这话时，他却无法接受。他只关注那些他没有救下的人。我和其他与大屠杀救援人员共事过或交谈过的人聊过，显然这是一个非常普遍的特征。这个世界的尼古拉斯·温顿，你知道的，那个因拯救了大约六百条生命而备受赞誉的英国人，所有那些参与其中的人

35
00:26:37.000 --> 00:27:22.000
in the Kindertransport, what preoccupied them was the ones left behind. That's what they kept thinking about. And Werber couldn't really think of himself as the hero of the 200,000. Rather, he was the one who had not done enough for the 437,000. Now, he didn't channel that all inward and blame himself. Instead, he was full of livid fury at those who had failed to pass on. His warning, top of his list, was the Hungarian Jewish leadership, Rezu Kastner, who he believed, as actually an Israeli court did find, had sold his soul to the devil by working with the Nazis.
在“儿童保育运输”中，让他们魂牵梦绕的是那些被留下的人。那是他们一直思考的事情。韦尔伯无法真正把自己视为那二十万人的英雄。相反，他是那个为那四万三千七百人做得不够的人。现在，他并没有把这一切都转向内心并责怪自己。相反，他对那些未能传递警告的人充满了极度的愤怒。他列出的黑名单之首就是匈牙利犹太人领导层鲁道夫·卡斯特纳，他认为——正如以色列法院确实裁定的那样——卡斯特纳通过与纳粹合作，把灵魂卖给了魔鬼。

36
00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:57.000
Again, highly contested. Others have a different view, but that's, you know, in a way, another story. But Verbe himself was convinced that he, as well as the allies, as well as other leaders in other places, had had the warning that he and Fred had risked everything to bring out and had not done enough with it. And the result was that Verbe himself became a figure that was, in the words of somebody who lived, you know, a fellow Jewish community member in Vancouver said, you know, we couldn't invite Rudy even to Holocaust memorial events in our town,
同样，这极具争议。其他人有不同的看法，但你知道，从某种程度上讲，这是另一个故事。但韦尔伯本人坚信，他以及盟友，还有其他地方的其他领导人，都收到了他和弗雷德冒着一切风险带出来的警告，却没有对它采取足够的行动。其结果是，韦尔伯本人变成了一个这样的人物：用一个在温哥华生活的人的话来说——一个犹太社区的同胞说，你知道的，我们甚至不能邀请鲁迪参加我们镇上的大屠杀纪念活动，

37
00:27:57.000 --> 00:28:42.000
because you did not know that he would not descend into, his words, accusations and rage. There was a symposium in Vancouver every year for high school students about the Holocaust. They had a panel of Holocaust survivors. And Rudolf Werber, the ultimate survivor who had seen everything in Auschwitz, was not invited because he was too angry to be a palatable figure for those high school kids. He wouldn't just say, you know, the evil people in this story is Adolf Hitler and the Nazis and everyone else was good, which is sort of what we want. Instead, he said there was culpability all around. But also, Jonathan, I mean, you call this book The Escape Artist, and there's a sense that even after he's escaped Auschwitz, his life
因为你不知道他会不会陷入——用他的话来说——指责和愤怒之中。温哥华每年都会为高中生举办一次关于大屠杀的研讨会。他们有一个大屠杀幸存者小组。而鲁道夫·韦尔伯，这个看透了奥斯威辛一切的终极幸存者，却没有被邀请，因为他太愤怒了，无法成为那些高中生眼中一个温和讨喜的人物。他不会只是说，你知道，这个故事里的坏人是阿道夫·希特勒和纳粹，其他人都是好人，这有点像我们想要的。相反，他说到处都有罪责。但是，乔纳森，我的意思是，你把这本书命名为《逃脱大师》，有一种感觉，即使在他逃离奥斯威辛之后，他的生活

38
00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:58.000
continues to be a process of flight. So he's in communist Czechoslovakia. He defects from there. He goes to England. He goes to Israel. He goes to Canada. He goes to America. And there seems to be a kind of perpetual restlessness. He separates from his wife.
仍然是一个不断逃亡的过程。所以他在共产主义捷克斯洛伐克。他从那里叛逃。他去了英国。他去了以色列。他去了加拿大。他去了美国。似乎有一种永无止境的动荡。他与妻子分居了。

39
00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:56.000
His elder daughter commits suicide. And it's evident that that shadow is never really escaped. I mean, I guess when you've lived through what he lived, I mean, it's just so unimaginable what the impact of it must have been that it must surely have shadowed him right up to the day of his death. his first wife. I think I mentioned when we spoke last time that I managed to track her down and find her just weeks before her death at age 93. And she told me about the marriage together and how terrible it was. It was so acrimonious, the divorce. The years later, she gave me actually a red suitcase full of his letters. And that was the moment when she passed that to me that I thought, okay, I am somehow meant to write this story. But what came through in those letters was tremendous acrimony and bitterness. But age 93, she was able to say, you know,
他的第一任妻子。我想我上次说话时提到过，设法追踪并找到了她，就在她 93 岁去世前几周。她告诉我他们在一起的婚姻以及它是多么糟糕。离婚是如此的充满怨恨。几年后，她实际上给了我一个装满他信件的红色手提箱。当她把那个交给我时，我想，好吧，我某种程度上注定要写这个故事。但那些信件中透露出的是巨大的怨恨和痛苦。但在 93 岁高龄时，她能够说，你知道，

40
00:29:56.000 --> 00:30:35.000
we were both so damaged. I mean, she was a Holocaust survivor too, in the sense she had been on the run in Slovakia. She'd never been in a camp, but she'd been in hiding. She'd lived on false papers and so on. We were both so damaged. And so no wonder that he was, for her, in her eyes, a terrible husband, an unfaithful husband, and this distrust, this paranoia, which had helped him in Auschwitz because he didn't trust the wrong people, made him impossible to live with, she said. All that said, he did then make a new life in Vancouver.
我们两个都受过太大的伤害。我的意思是，她也是大屠杀幸存者，从某种意义上说，她在斯洛伐克一直在逃亡。她从未进过集中营，但她一直在躲藏。她使用假身份证件生活等等。“我们两个都受得伤太重了。难怪在她的眼中，他是一个糟糕的丈夫，一个不忠的丈夫，这种不信任，这种偏执——这在奥斯威辛救了他，因为他不信任错误的人——让她无法与之共处，”她说。尽管如此，他后来在温哥华开创了新生活。

41
00:30:35.000 --> 00:31:13.000
He married again a second wife who was, and I don't think this is a coincidence, a whole generation younger and from a continent away from the Holocaust. She was an American who, when he met her, was 24 years old, utterly unmarked by the trauma of Europe, as he would have felt it. And he was there on the far west coast of Canada. I think these things are not coincidences. And she described a man who could be playful, who took great pleasure in the very simple things in life that, you know, enjoyed being inside when it was raining, would look outside and say, isn't that wonderful?
他又结了婚，娶了第二任妻子，我不认为这是一个巧合，她比他年轻整整一代，而且来自一个远离大屠杀的大陆。她是一个美国人，当他遇到她时 24 岁，完全没有受到欧洲创伤的印记，就像他所感受到的那样。而他当时在加拿大遥远的西海岸。我认为这些事情绝非巧合。她描述了一个能够嬉戏玩耍的男人，他在生活中非常简单的事情中获得巨大的快乐，你知道，下雨天喜欢呆在室内，会看着外面说：“那不是很棒吗？”

42
00:31:13.000 --> 00:32:01.000
Because this is somebody who had been naked in the frozen waste of my Danek or Auschwitz. He could enjoy practical jokes and he would sort of be quite goofy. And she describes a man who somehow was able to find a new life. He was very committed as a scientist, you know, as a biochemist, teaching at the University of British Columbia. And he would be very insistent, oh, you know, Auschwitz and the Holocaust takes up 1% of my time, no more. Nevertheless, I've gone through the papers, files upon files upon files of material on this subject. He would be testifying at war crimes trials. He testified against a notorious Holocaust denier in Canada. He was on the stand for days. When he was ill, finally with a terminal
因为这是一个曾在马伊达内克（Majdanek）或奥斯威辛的冰冻荒原中赤身裸体的人。他可以享受恶作剧，并且会表现得相当滑稽。她描述了一个不知何故能够找到新生活的男人。他作为一名科学家非常投入，你知道，作为一名生物化学家，在不列颠哥伦比亚大学（University of British Columbia）教书。他会非常坚持地说：“哦，你知道的，奥斯威辛和大屠杀只占我时间的 1%，绝不多。”尽管如此，我还是浏览了关于这个主题的文件、一份又一份的文件、一堆又一堆的资料。他将在战争罪审判中作证。他在加拿大指证了一名臭名昭著的大屠杀否定论者。他在证人席上呆了好几天。当他生病时，最后患上了晚期……

43
00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:54.000
illness, he said to a friend when the illness had got the better of him, the Gestapo have finally caught up with me. When he was living in England, he drove past Battersea power station, saw the chimneys and said, it reminds me of Auschwitz. Of course he couldn't escape it. That shadow was there all the time. I found in his papers a birthday greeting he had sent to his youngest daughter when she turned 44. And he reminded her that 44 was his lucky number. Why? Because his Auschwitz number was 44070. He thought 44 was his lucky number because that was part of the number he'd had in Auschwitz. So this is a complicated man, you know, in every conceivable way, who both found a new life, and yet at every turn,
病情恶化时，他对一位朋友说，盖世太保最终还是追上我了。当他住在英国时，他开车路过巴特西发电厂，看到烟囱便说，这让我想起了奥斯威辛。当然，他是无法逃避这一切的。那种阴影一直挥之不去。我在他的文件中发现了一封生日贺信，那是他写给小女儿的，当时她44岁。他提醒女儿，44是他的幸运数字。为什么？因为他在奥斯威辛的编号是44070。他认为44是他的幸运数字，因为那是他在奥斯威辛编号的一部分。所以这是一个复杂的人，你知道，在任何可以想象的方面，他既找到了新生活，但在每一个转折点，

44
00:32:54.000 --> 00:33:59.000
Auschwitz was the event which had shaped him. And of course, how could it be any other way? This is a massive question, Jonathan, but do you think part of the reason that he's not so well known is that we expect Holocaust survivors to be martyrs only, to be kind of saintly? And he was too spiky. He's too difficult. He doesn't fit in. We have now, I mean, we can get into this issue of where the Holocaust stands in that kind of moral consciousness as this kind of almost sacred transcendent moment. But he doesn't fit that, does he? view that we are very unfair in what we demand of Holocaust survivors, but I would also say it goes wider of survivors of great traumatic events, that we do endow them with a kind of saintliness, and we want them, therefore, to dispense a kind of consoling, healing wisdom to us,
奥斯威辛是塑造他一生的事件。当然，怎么可能不是呢？这是一个巨大的问题，乔纳森，但你认为他不那么广为人知的另一个原因，是不是因为我们期望犹太人大屠杀的幸存者只能是烈士，只能像圣人一样？而他太特立独行了。他太难对付了。他格格不入。我们现在——我的意思是，我们可以探讨大屠杀在道德意识中处于什么位置，作为一个几乎神圣的、超越的时刻。但他不符合这一点，对吧？对于我们对大屠杀幸存者提出的要求，我们的看法非常不公平，但我也要说，这种现象不仅限于大屠杀，对于经历过巨大创伤事件的幸存者也是如此，我们赋予了他们一种圣洁感，因此我们希望他们能向我们散发一种安慰、治愈的智慧，

45
00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:20.000
as if their touch will somehow sanctify us. And, you know, there's very, very few Holocaust survivors left now. The numbers are very dwindling. But when you hear them interviewed on the radio, the interviewer adopts a tone of voice they also have with kind of Dalai Lama or something, you know, this sort of hushed reverence. And they want an uplifting experience.
仿佛他们的抚摸能以某种方式净化我们。而且，你知道，现在幸存下来的大屠杀幸存者已经非常非常少了。人数正在急剧减少。但当你听到他们在电台上接受采访时，采访者采用了一种对待达赖喇嘛或类似人物时才有的语调，你知道的，那种压低声音的敬畏。他们想要一种精神振奋的体验。

46
00:34:20.000 --> 00:35:08.000
And I think this is such an unfair demand to make. And Rudy was so aware of it. I found in his papers a letter he wrote to a BBC producer. The BBC producer had invited him to come on a TV documentary. And Rudy says, I must warn you, I am not the cliched Holocaust survivor. I won't be that person for you. I'm going to be spiky and awkward and difficult. And the main way I'm going to be difficult is I'm going to tell you that there is guilt to go around here. And everyone you think is the heroes of this story, and that might be Jewish leaders, but it might be Roosevelt, it might be Churchill. Well, it's not always as simple as that. You know, he was keen and he was very tailored, by the way, I should say this to his audience. When he was in Germany, he absolutely pointed the finger at Germany and Nazis and Hitler and was very clear on it.
我认为这是一个非常不公平的要求。鲁迪对此非常清楚。我在他的文件中发现了他写给BBC制片人的一封信。BBC制片人邀请他参加一个电视纪录片。鲁迪说，我必须警告你，我不是那种老套的大屠杀幸存者。我不会为你扮演那种角色。我会表现得尖锐、尴尬和难以相处。而我最难以相处的地方在于，我要告诉你，这里每个人都有罪。你认为这个故事中的英雄——可能犹太领袖是，但也可能是罗斯福、丘吉尔。嗯，事情并没有那么简单。你知道，顺便说一句，我很想对他的听众说，他很敏锐，而且很有针对性。当他在德国时，他明确地把矛头指向德国、纳粹和希特勒，态度非常明确。

47
00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:43.000
But if he was talking to Americans or Brits, he would mention the failures in London and Washington and so on. He was difficult. And I think that's why he wasn't famous. He didn't get the invitation even in Vancouver. But that's also why he was not invited to all the big conferences and elevated into that stature. I think it's really telling that the really very best, in my view, chroniclers of the Holocaust sought him out. They understood how important he was. Claude Lansman puts him as a key witness in the film Shoah. Martin Gilbert spoke to him
但如果他跟美国人或英国人交谈，他就会提到伦敦和华盛顿等地的失职。他很难对付。我想这就是他为什么不出名的原因。他甚至在温哥华都没有收到邀请。但这也是他没有被邀请参加所有大型会议并被推崇到那种地位的原因。我觉得非常能说明问题的是，在我看来，那些真正最优秀的大屠杀记录者都曾找过他。他们明白他有多重要。克劳德·兰斯曼将他视为电影《浩劫》中的关键见证人。马丁·吉尔伯特经常与他交谈，

48
00:35:43.000 --> 00:36:26.000
often for his book, Auschwitz and the Allies, and other books. The top two or three people got it, but I think other people thought, difficult character, let's steer clear. But Jonathan, to go back to Auschwitz, you have this terrifying description of what the inhabitants of Auschwitz looked like and how, and to Walter, to Rudy, they don't seem human. The living dead, walking skeletons with bowed heads and sunken hollow eyes, non-men whose muscle and flesh had wasted away, who were expiring in plain sight, the breath of life leaving them slowly but inexorably. And I think that is how, perhaps in the imagination, that is how we think of the people who die in Auschwitz,
为了他的书《奥斯威辛与盟军》以及其他书籍。前两三个人看出来了，但我认为其他人会想，这是一个难搞的角色，我们还是离远点吧。但是，乔纳森，回到奥斯威辛，你对奥斯威辛居民的长相有着可怕的描述，以及——对沃尔特和鲁迪来说，他们看起来并不像人。行尸走肉，低着头、眼眶深陷的骷髅，那些肌肉和血肉已经消瘦殆尽、非人的生灵，他们在光天化日之下消逝，生命的气息缓缓但不可阻挡地从他们身上离去。我想，也许在想象中，这就是我们认为死在奥斯威辛的人的方式，

49
00:36:26.000 --> 00:37:18.000
is as people in a sense who are already dead, who have already passed into a dimension of shadow. It's because Rudy is such a distinct individual. He's so alive. He's not a ghoul. He's not a thing of shadow. He is a living, breathing person. But that, it seems to me, is why your book, I am sure, you say that you want him to rank alongside Anne Frank and Primo Levi, but I'm sure your book will rank alongside Anne Frank's diary and Primo Levi's book as a record of what it was like to be human amid all that suffering, of what it's like to be a living individual amid the six million. Because that is the problem, isn't it? With making sense of the Holocaust, of fathoming the Holocaust, of presenting the Holocaust as a field of study, is how do you
把他们看作某种意义上已经死去的人，已经进入阴影维度的人。正因为鲁迪是一个如此鲜明的个体。他是如此充满生机。他不是一个食尸鬼。他不是阴影中的事物。他是一个活生生、有呼吸的人。但在我看来，这就是为什么你的书——我确信，你说你希望他能与安妮·弗兰克和普里莫·莱维并列——我确信你的书将与安妮·弗兰克的日记和普里莫·莱维的书并列，成为在所有苦难中保持人性是什么感觉的记录，成为在六百万人中作为一个活生生的个体的记录。因为这就是问题所在，不是吗？要理解大屠杀，要探究大屠杀，要将大屠杀作为一门研究领域呈现出来，问题就在于，你如何

50
00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:43.000
extract individuals from the totality of those millions? I mean, even doing this, Tom, you know, when Jonathan says 15,000 a day, 20,000 a day, you have to do a bit of work. You have to translate it into something concrete. And that's quite difficult to do. And I agree with you. I think, I mean, well, are you conscious of this, Jonathan? Do you think we dehumanize the Holocaust almost by turning it into numbers rather than
从数百万人的整体中提取出个体？我的意思是，汤姆，即使在做这件事时，你知道的，当乔纳森说一天一万五千人、一天两万人时，你必须做一些工作。你必须把它转化为具体的东西。这其实很难做到。我同意你的看法。我的意思是，乔纳森，你意识到这一点吗？你认为我们通过把大屠杀变成数字，而不是

51
